  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for moderate to heavy rainfall and a \nfew storms tonight. Widespread rain and drizzle for Wednesday \ninto Thursday.\n\nFrontal boundary has worked its way back north this afternoon,\ncurrently situated from southeast West Virginia, then east \nacross Roanoke and into northern North Carolina. With the front\nnearby, this should aid in supporting a few thunderstorms this \nafternoon along the periphery of the front. Severe storms not \nexpected, but given the pulse-like nature of the convection, a \nfew brief stronger cells cannot be ruled out. Some of the\nshowers have been heavy at times, and are slow moving, so\nponding of water on roadways and overflowing of poor drainage\nareas are possible. Be extra cautious driving at night, as it\ncan be harder to see water on the road.\n\nOvernight, the frontal boundary will again move southward with a\nstrong northeast push of cool maritime air in response to a\ndeveloping surface low over the Atlantic. Increasing isentropic\nlift should result in rain showers developing overnight and \ninto Wednesday as the gradient strengthens between high \npressure over southern Canada and low pressure in the Atlantic.\nCool and damp conditions will continue into Thursday, slowly \nimproving into Friday as flow becomes more northwesterly, then \nbecoming dry and mild for the weekend.\n\nHave continued to lower temperatures a few degrees below\nguidance for Weds/Thu due to the persistent cold air damming\nsignal. Highs will be below average in the 50s and 60s both\ndays..falling into the low 50s to upper 40s overnight.",
